// **"Store less, compute again" is only safe when running the body twice cannot be seen.**
//
// This program is the pin for that rule: `g` has an external effect (`println` in its body) and is
// tagged `#remat`, so `jvp`'s lift refuses to carry the recompute bit and says why --
// `REINTERP jvp not_recomputed=g why=an operator of the body has an external effect, so it would run
// twice`. The cut then stops at a different, earlier wall (`split does not build this function`), which
// is what the expected reading below records.
//
// **Measured 2026-11-04**: the wall below is **not** `split does not build this function` any more. The
// cut of this body builds (`nodes=11 exit=10`, the graph `uses stdout`) and the program stops in the
// scheduler instead: `Pass 'std::nvm' failed with an internal error: Runtime graph 'jvp_of_g' has GATE node
// 10 without a wait: no sync predecessor and no control input`. That is the "if `split` ever starts
// accepting an effectful body" case this comment asks about, and it arrived: what the cut emits does not
// serve the GATE the effect put in the body. Read the paragraph below with that in hand.
//
// **The assertion to come back to**: if `split` ever starts accepting an effectful body, this case will
// go red first. Then read this paragraph -- the reason the bit was withheld has not gone away, and the
// question to ask is whether the cut now has its own answer to "can this be run twice", not how to make
// the case pass again.
//
// The cut is asked with the closure ABI (`let fwd = split(jvp(g))`, then `let (y, lin) = fwd(x)`), so
// the reading below is the refusal itself, not a mis-spelled request.
import rulesets.jvp
import { jvp, split } from transforms

#remat
func g(x: float): float sync {
    println("side")
    return x * x * x
}

func main(): int sync {
    let fwd = split(jvp(g))
    let (_y, lin) = fwd(3.0)
    println("dx=", lin(1.0))
    return 0
}